Based on the provided context, you can manually trigger event handlers (such as a click event on a button) using the **`.trigger()`** method. 

To do this, you first attach an event handler to your element using the `.on()` method (or one of its shortcut methods) and then call `.trigger()` with the event type. 

**Example from the context:**
```javascript
$( "#foo" ).on( "click" , function ( ) { 
    alert( $( this ).text() ); 
}); 

$( "#foo" ).trigger( "click" );
```

**Alternative:**
If you want to trigger the handlers bound via jQuery *without* also triggering the native event, you should use the **`.triggerHandler()`** method instead.
